# A source distribution has to carry enough to rebuild and re-verify the
# project, and this one did not. setuptools' default sdist file list includes
# `tests/test*.py` -- a distutils legacy rule -- and nothing else, and there was
# no MANIFEST.in, so the released 0.7.0 sdist shipped 18 of the 20 files in
# tests/: `conftest.py` and `__init__.py` do not match `test*.py`. Every module
# importing a fixture from `tests.conftest` then failed at collection --
#
#   $ tar xzf slurmpast-0.7.0.tar.gz && cd slurmpast-0.7.0 && pytest -q
#   ERROR tests/test_extraction.py ... ERROR tests/test_sizing.py
#   E   ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'tests.conftest'
#   Interrupted: 5 errors during collection
#
# -- and once that was fixed, eight more failed for the same underlying reason
# one level out: this suite audits the repository, not only the package. It
# checks the README against the dependency pin in `.github/workflows/ci.yml`,
# the prose in `docs/details.md` against the code it documents, the import
# declarations in `tools/`, and every image `README.md` points at under
# `assets/`. None of those shipped either.
#
# That matters for this package specifically: the way its portability claims get
# checked is somebody on a cluster nobody here has seen downloading the
# *released* artefact and running it there. A suite that cannot be collected --
# or that fails eight tests on a correct build -- reads as a broken package
# rather than a broken sdist.
#
# Grafting whole directories rather than naming files, so a helper added later
# is not silently left behind the same way.
graft tests
graft docs
graft tools
graft assets
graft .github
include LICENSE
include README.md
include issues.md
